@page "/privacy"
@inject ErCvOptions Options

<PageTitle>@($"Privacy - {UiConstants.AppName}")</PageTitle>

@* OPS-05. Every statement here is checked against the code: update this page when what is stored or sent changes. *@
<div class="container app-shell py-4 py-lg-5 privacy-page">
    <div class="d-flex flex-wrap justify-content-between align-items-center gap-2 mb-3">
        <h1 class="privacy-page-title">Privacy</h1>
        <a class="btn btn-outline-secondary btn-sm" href="/">Back to the generator</a>
    </div>
    <p class="privacy-page-intro">
        There is no account and no database. Your data lives in your own browser, and the server only holds it in
        memory while it builds your CV.
    </p>

    <section class="card main-card" aria-labelledby="privacy-browser">
        <div class="card-body form-card-body">
            <h2 class="privacy-section-title" id="privacy-browser">Saved in your browser</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>Your settings: template, formats, page mode, language, accent, fonts and theme.</li>
                <li>Your generation history, up to 50 entries, with each job description, the company name (the one you typed in step 1 or the one read from the post) and the application details you add.</li>
                <li>The profile you save on <a href="/profile">My profile</a>.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                They stay in this browser's local storage and no other device sees them. Remove them with
                <strong>Clear</strong> in the history, <strong>Clear my profile</strong> on My profile, or by clearing
                this site's data in your browser.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="card main-card mt-3" aria-labelledby="privacy-server">
        <div class="card-body form-card-body">
            <h2 class="privacy-section-title" id="privacy-server">What the server sees</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>When you generate a CV, the job description, the company name if there is one and the profile are sent to the server to build it. The company is only used to name it in the cover letter.</li>
                <li>When you read a job post from its Greenhouse or Lever link, the server asks that job board's public API for the post. Only the board and the post id from your link are sent to it. The API can do the same for a link sent to it (<code>/api/cv/import-job-post</code>).</li>
                <li>When you start your profile from your resume, the file is sent to the server, read in memory and dropped. Only the fields read from it come back to the page; nothing is kept.</li>
                <li>While this page is open, the server keeps the generated CV in memory so you can edit and download it. It is gone when you close the page.</li>
                <li>Through the API, the generated files stay in a server-side session for up to @Options.Session.IdleTimeoutMinutes minutes of inactivity, so they can be downloaded.</li>
                <li>Nothing is written to disk or to a database.</li>
                <li>Error logs hold technical details only (for example a date the parser could not read), never your whole CV or the job description.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="card main-card mt-3" aria-labelledby="privacy-third-parties">
        <div class="card-body form-card-body">
            <h2 class="privacy-section-title" id="privacy-third-parties">No tracking</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>No analytics and no ads. Fonts, styles and scripts are served by the app itself.</li>
                <li>Cookies: only the ones the app needs to work (the session and the anti-forgery cookie).</li>
                <li>The hosting provider may keep standard access logs (IP address, time and page requested).</li>
                <li>The sample job descriptions are public job posts, each linked to the original.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>
</div>
